# Code owners for the paths that gate everything else (MDRS-49).
#
# STATUS: ADVISORY, NOT ENFORCED. The "main protection" ruleset on this repo
# (id 20827887) requires a pull request with 1 approval and the "Verify" and
# "Commit hygiene" status checks, but it does NOT set
# `require_code_owner_review`. So today this file only auto-requests these
# reviewers; any single approval still satisfies the rule. Turning it into a
# requirement is a repo-settings change, made deliberately and separately.
#
# These paths are the ones that can disable the other gates — a change to
# .github/ can drop a required check, a change to tools/ci/ can make a gate pass
# vacuously — so review of them must never be single-threaded on one person's
# availability.
#
# WHO IS LISTED, AND WHY THESE FOUR (MDRS-54). The owners are the current
# write-or-above collaborators with real history on these paths, measured rather
# than assumed. Commits touching .github/workflows/ across the full history:
# Taha 26, safakadir 25, Samet 12, Onurkan 9, Enes 7, zahidcakici 4 — and on the
# per-app deploy workflows specifically, safakadir 5, Onurkan 4, Samet 3, Taha 2,
# Enes 1. The previous two-name list did not include the second and third most
# active people on these files. Two of the authors above are not eligible:
# onurkanbakirci has read access only and zahidcakici is not a collaborator.
#
# THE HANDLE MATTERS. This file used to name @tahaayan; Taha authors pull
# requests as @EsTharian. Both are his real accounts, which is why the mistake
# was silent — GitHub never recognised the author as an owner, so it never
# skipped him, it just requested the other name every time.
#
# COST OF ADDING A NAME. Individual owners are ALL requested on every matching
# pull request; the author is skipped, so four names means three notifications.
# If that becomes noisy the fix is not a shorter list — it is naming the
# @amel-tech/medaris team (9 members, exactly the write+ collaborators) and
# enabling GitHub's code review assignment to round-robin to N reviewers. That
# is an org/team setting, not a change to this file.
#
# GitHub applies the LAST matching rule, so order is specific-after-general.

# Everything else has no owner: reviewer choice stays open for ordinary app and
# library work, which is where most PRs land.

